# kanso play bugs/param_named_done.kso
#
# SHOULD: print "abc", or be refused at compile time for naming a parameter
# after the `done` marker, the way a local binding named after a declared
# type is refused ("`x` is already a declaration; rename the binding").
#
# DOES: checks clean, then fails at run time on the interpreter with
#   error[runtime]: no overload of `sel` matches these arguments
# because the parameter `done` is read as the marker pattern `done` (the
# value a finished write yields), not as a name. `sel` has exactly one
# arm, and nothing in the message points at the parameter. Rename the
# parameter to `finish` and it prints "abc". `done` is the natural name for
# a continuation, which is how xsv/selection.kso met it.
fn go s
  sel s (xs -> "{xs}")

fn sel s done
  done s

print (go "abc")
